# {{projectName}} - Built with nutin

## Start

- `{{packageManager}} run serve` (rebuild), `{{packageManager}} run serve:only` (no rebuild), `{{packageManager}} run dev` (watch).

## Build

- `{{packageManager}} run build` / `{{packageManager}} run build:prod`

## Generate Component, View, Service

- `{{packageManager}} run generate ELEMENT ELEMENT_NAME`

Supports nested paths. Updates element's barrel file (`src/app/ELEMENT/index.ts`).

## Add feature

```bash
# Features: accessibility-components, forms, overlays, nutin-mixins, docker
nutin-add <feature> 

nutin-add libs # accessibility-components, forms, overlays, nutin-mixins
nutin-add all
```

## Configuration

### `nutin.config.js`

Central build/feature config (see the inline comments for details):
- `inlineTemplates`, `i18n`, `tailwind` — top-level feature toggles.
- `generator.{generateStylesheet, generateLocales, generateTest}` — what the generator scaffolds alongside new elements.
- `builder.{generateSEO, logLevel, sass.paths, esbuild, compression}` — build pipeline behavior.
- `testinNutin.{includeFramework, includeTools, includeApp, verbose, jsdomOptions}` — test discovery/environment.

### `config/seo.json`

- Register your routes' description, title and ogImage here.
- Consumed by builder (production only) to generate static HTML per route.
- Unused if `nutin.config.js -> generateSEO` is disabled.

### `config/languages.json`

- Register supported languages for i18n here.
- Unused if `nutin.config.js -> i18n` is disabled.

## Update Nutin version

```bash
nutin-update # minor / patch.
```

Consumes `.nutin-meta.json`.
- If changes were made to src/core/ or src/libs/, will prompt and report related files diffs in a markdown file, and update untouched ones.
- If no changes were made to src/core/ or src/libs/, will update Nutin files.

## Testing

You are free to use the testing tool you prefer for your application — Jest, Vitest, or another solution.                                    

This project includes TestinNutin, Nutin's lightweight testing toolkit, and *Nutin ships its own tests* with high coverage so that people modifying the toolkit can verify that their changes haven't introduced regressions.                                                  

These tests serve two purposes:
- Executable documentation: they show how Nutin's core and libraries are expected to behave.
- Framework validation: they allow you to run and modify Nutin's own tests when customizing the framework.                                           

**Run Nutin tests with: `{{packageManager}} run testin-nutin`.**

*If you choose to use TestinNutin for your own app, simply enable it in `nutin.config.js -> testinNutin -> includeApp: true`.* Its syntax is intentionally lightweight and familiar to developers coming from Jest and similar tools.

